          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em classifying topos
            for a given type of mathematical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            — for example the structures: “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"}> group
            ”, “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"}> torsor
            ”, “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"}> ring
            ”, “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"}> category
            ” etc. — is a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"}> Grothendieck
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S[T]
            such that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: E \to S[T]
            are the same as structures of this sort in the topos
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representing+object"} representing object
            for the functor which sends a topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            to the category of structures of the desired sort in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            In particular for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            a (bare, i.e. discrete)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "-torsor" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mi Bund
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G Bund(X)
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is equivalent to geometric morphims
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X) \to B G
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mi Bund
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Topos
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G Bund(X) \simeq Topos(Sh(X), B G)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is evidently analogous to the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{B} G
            such that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mi G
                  %mi Bund
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mi Top
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\pi_0 G Bund(X) \simeq \pi_0 Top(X, \mathcal{B}G)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Hence one can think of classifying topoi as a grand generalization of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            in topology.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            In a tautological way, every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is the classifying topos for something, namely for the categories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to F
            into it. The concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
            allows one to usefully interpret these categories as
            %em
              categories of certain structures in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            \:
          %p
            as decribed in
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a{:href => "geometric+theory#InTermsOfSheafTopoi"} Geometric theories – In terms of sheaf topoi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a completion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S[T]
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
            of
            %em some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F \simeq S[T]
                  \\,.
          %p
            And structures of type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is what geometric morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to F
            classify.
          %p
            So the
            %strong
              classifying
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S[T]
            equipped with a “universal model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            of
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            together with a model
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: E \to S[T]
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            maps the
            = succeed "-model" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            to the model
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            is equivalent to the category of geometric morphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to S[T]
          %p
            The fact that a classifying topos is like the ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            but equipped with that universal model is essentially the notion of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forcing"} forcing
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of the classifying topos
            %em forces
            the universal model to exist.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+category"} geometric functors
            out of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U : C_T \to S[T]
                  \\,.
          %p Its universality property means that any geometric functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X : C_T \to E
          %p factors essentially uniquely as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi T
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo *
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X : C_T \stackrel{U}{\to} S[T] \stackrel{f^*}{\to} E
          %p
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            the universal model and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            part of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            defines an equivalence between the category of geometric morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\to S[T]
            and the category of geometric functors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_T\to E
          %p
            More specifically, for any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+theory"} cartesian theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+theory"} regular theory
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+theory"} coherent theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
            (which in ascending order are special cases of each other and all of geometric theories), the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{\mathbb{T}}
            comes equipped with the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+site"} syntactic site
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝕋
                  %mo ,
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{C},\mathbb{T}, J)
            (see there) and the classifying topos for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕋
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{T}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mi 𝕋
                    %mo ,
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(\mathcal{C}_{\mathbb{T}}, J)
          %p
            Classifying toposes can also be defined over any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"} base topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            instead of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+topos"} bounded
            = succeed "-topos\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %em general existence of classifying toposes
            for geometric theories for bounded
            = succeed "-toposes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos+for+the+theory+of+objects"} classifying topos for the theory of objects
            which in turn hinges on the existence of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number+object"} natural number object
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a{:href => "#ForObjects"} below
            and, for further details,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+topos+for+the+theory+of+objects"} classifying topos for the theory of objects
            or Blass (
            %a{:href => "#blass"}> 1989
            ).
          %p
            If the classifying topos of a geometric theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+topos"} presheaf topos
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+presheaf+type"} theory of presheaf type
          %h2#background_on_the_theory_of_theories Background on the theory of theories
          %p
            The notion of
            %em classifying topos
            is part of a trend, begun by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Lawvere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            with suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+functor"} exactness
            properties and which contains a “generic model”, and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model"} model
            of the theory as a functor which preserves those properties. This is described in more detail at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong Finite products theory.
                Roughly speaking, a ‘finite products theory’, ‘
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"}> Lawvere theory
                ’, or ‘
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"}> algebraic theory
                ’ is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
                describing some mathematical structure that can be defined in an arbitrary category with finite
                = succeed "s." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product"} product
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                there is a category with finite products
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi fp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{fp}[T]
                – the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} syntactic category
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi T
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                in the category of sets correspond to product-preserving
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi fp
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo →
                        %mi Set
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : C_{fp}[T] \to Set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                correspond to product-preserving functors
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi fp
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo →
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : C_{fp}[T] \to E
            %li
              %p
                %strong Finite limits theory.
                Next up the line is the notion of ‘finite limits theory’, sometimes called an
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"} essentially algebraic theory
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+limit"} finite limit
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete category
                %em partially defined
                operation, namely composition of morphisms.) Every finite limits theory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                admits a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+category"} classifying category
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi C
                          %mi fl
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{fl}(T)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                in a category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                with finite limits correspond to functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi fl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi E
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: C_{fl}(T) \to E
                that preserve finite limits. (Such functors are called
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+exact"} left exact
            %li
              %p
                %strong Geometric theory.
                Further up the line, a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+theory"} geometric theory
                is roughly a theory which can be formulated in that fragment of first-order logic that deals in finite limits and arbitrary (small) colimits, plus certain
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/familial+regularity+and+exactness"} exactness
                properties the details of which need not concern us. The point is that a category with finite limits, small colimits, and appropriate exactness is just a Grothendieck topos, and a functor preserving finite limits and small colimits is just the inverse image part of a geometric morphism. Just as in the previous two cases, any ‘geometric theory’ has a classifying category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S[T]
                (which is now a Grothendieck topos) which possesses a “generic object” for that theory, and T-models in any other Grothendieck topos E can be identified with geometric morphisms
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi E
                        %mo →
                        %mi S
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon E\to S[T]
          %p
            Each type of theory may be considered a
            = succeed "-theory," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} doctrine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion"} freely adding
            the missing structure to the classifying category. This can always be done purely formally, but in a few cases this promotion also has other, more explicit descriptions.
          %p
            For instance, to go from a finite products theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            to the corresponding finite limits theory, we can take the opposite of the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+presentable+object"} finitely presentable models
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Ulmer+duality"} Gabriel-Ulmer duality
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#GeometricMorphismsAndMorphismsOfSites Geometric morphisms equivalent to morphisms of sites
          %p
            The fact that classifying toposes are what they are all comes down, if spelled out explicitly, to the fact that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphism"} geometric morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathcal{E} \to \mathcal{F}
            of toposes can be identified with a certain morphism of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{\mathcal{E}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_{\mathcal{F}}
            for these toposes, going the other way round,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi ℰ
                    %mo ←
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\mathcal{E} \leftarrow C_{\mathcal{F}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi C
                    %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\mathcal{F}
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntactic+site"} syntactic site
